{"id": "572ab969111d821400f38cf2", "group_id": "Royal_assent", "input": "Article: Royal_assent\nPassage: If the Spanish monarch ever refused in conscience to grant royal assent, a procedure similar to the Belgian handling of King Baudouin's objection would not be possible under the current constitution. If the sovereign were ever declared incapable of discharging royal authority, his or her powers would not be transferred to the Cabinet, pending the parliamentary appointment of a regency. Instead, the constitution mandates the next person of age in the line of succession would immediately become regent. Therefore, had Juan Carlos followed the Belgian example in 2005 or 2010, a declaration of incapacity would have transferred power to Felipe, then the heir apparent.\nQuestion: If the Spanish monarch is deemed incapable of granting assent, who is the power transferred to?"}
