ture of Knowledge: Thoughts on 1848\") (1848–49), states: \"It is my deep conviction that pure humanism will be the religion of the future, that is, the cult of all that pertains to humanity—all of life, sanctified and raised to the level of a moral value.\"\nQuestion: Who felt that humanism would surely be a major \"religion\" today?"}
