{"id": "572970aaaf94a219006aa413", "group_id": "United_States_presidential_election,_2004", "input": "Article: United_States_presidential_election,_2004\nPassage: A ballot initiative in Colorado, known as Amendment 36, would have changed the way in which the state apportions its electoral votes. Rather than assigning all 9 of the state's electors to the candidate with a plurality of popular votes, under the amendment Colorado would have assigned presidential electors proportionally to the statewide vote count, which would be a unique system (Nebraska and Maine assign electoral votes based on vote totals within each congressional district). Detractors claimed that this splitting would diminish Colorado's influence in the Electoral College, and the amendment ultimately failed, receiving only 34% of the vote.\nQuestion: What action suggested by a state, would have affecting the outcome of the electoral votes?"}
