{"id": "57310cad05b4da19006bcd2a", "group_id": "Immaculate_Conception", "input": "Article: Immaculate_Conception\nPassage: The popularity of this particular representation of The Immaculate Conception spread across the rest of Europe, and has since remained the best known artistic depiction of the concept: in a heavenly realm, moments after her creation, the spirit of Mary (in the form of a young woman) looks up in awe at (or bows her head to) God. The moon is under her feet and a halo of twelve stars surround her head, possibly a reference to \"a woman clothed with the sun\" from Revelation 12:1-2. Additional imagery may include clouds, a golden light, and cherubs. In some paintings the cherubim are holding lilies and roses, flowers often associated with Mary.\nQuestion: What became very popular symbol among the who believed Mary had a Virgin for a mother ? "}
