{"id": "570b9fa36b8089140040f9c0", "group_id": "Infrared", "input": "Article: Infrared\nPassage: The sensitivity of Earth-based infrared telescopes is significantly limited by water vapor in the atmosphere, which absorbs a portion of the infrared radiation arriving from space outside of selected atmospheric windows. This limitation can be partially alleviated by placing the telescope observatory at a high altitude, or by carrying the telescope aloft with a balloon or an aircraft. Space telescopes do not suffer from this handicap, and so outer space is considered the ideal location for infrared astronomy.\nQuestion: What limits the sensitivity of infrared telescopes on Earth?"}
